ly things are a little more  complicated than they previously thought  once they get into this big ol hole this  entire special has a really nostalgic  vibe to it and one that i really really  liked  similar to the very first movie of the  one piece series i covered  it only has the east blue crew prior to  ever recruiting chopper or anyone else  thereafter  its animation is nostalgic humble and  quaint and it starts with luffy peeing  off the side of the ship  funny imagery aside however this begins  with a really characterful and funny set  of scenes  luffy is fishing for something big off  the side nami asks what he's doing and  then something big begins pulling the  ship  luffy thinking it's a massive catch  starts pulling everything and the ship  along to that destination while nami and  everyone wonder what the heck is going  on they finally reached the end and it's  a treasure chest luffy  immeasurably disappointed throws the  chest away citing that it tasted  awful prompting nami to then jump into  the water after it getting angry at  luffy for throwing it away  i loved this interaction because it only  works if you understand these characters  it's simple but it's entertaining  character writing it's compelling the  chest itself leads nami usa and sanji  down into the depths below in search of  a treasure  and just when i thought we were getting  away from this discovering a new island  trope in  every one of these standalone films what  do we get  in the depths of the ocean another  island  i actually really like the vibe in this  story it's easily the funniest out of  all of them that i've seen so far  has a great sense of exploration and  discovery while having a strong focus on  character  everything that happens in it happens as  a result of who these characters are how  do they find this location  luffy and nami being luffy and nami how  do they get down there luffy and usop  how do they survive and get discovered  sanji usaf and zaro all contributing in  their own unique way  that only makes sense if they do it this  is compelling writing 101 and while the  plot is simple  it very much is a story i can get behind  and enjoy  specifically because i'm a fan of the  series the second half of the story  however is a slightly more traditional  one-piece special in that they identify  a big bad and get to know the local  inhabitants and their struggles before  helping them thereafter they're scary  spooky skeleton zombie men pirates  talking bats and a plot involving this  young boy that's quite emotionally rich  too considering the time spent on the  actual topic  by the time i reached the end this felt  like all the best parts of thriller bark  meets the intro sequence of fishman  island crossed with indiana jones  it's fun the action isn't as strong as  the new more flashy films or specials  but the passion put into the writing for  this one is clear  it's a wonderful fun romp that isn't too  long and offers more bang for his buck  than most of these specials could even  hope to incorporate  i'm a huge fan of this one check it out  episode of sabo  if any of you out there someday decide  to tackle all of the one piece tv  specials like i did  you will no doubt come into contact  quite quickly with the likes of what i  call  the episode of series there are six one  piece tv specials affectionately titled  the episode of nami luffy the east blue  the mary's  skypia and in this case sabo every one  of these details previously told stories  in miniature  sort of like a speedrun of a particular  arc episode of nami details the ireland  park arc episode of skype and east blue  obviously cover their respective arcs  and episode of mary covers the water  seven  in these lobby arcs however episode of  luffy and episode of sabo while covering  previously released storylines like  luffy's origin and sabo's origin  and sabo's overall participation in the  dressrosa arc they also do so in tandem  with new material which is wha