as a  dragon ball fan that enjoys training  arcs  even getting a glimpse at that  progression is enough for me to whet my  appetite with one piece is a great  series but one aspect i would have liked  to have seen more of was how the  different characters came up with and  developed their techniques this film  gave me that or  at the very least an indication of what  happened  it's a very fun story with a strong  emotional core and what's more  is the animation is bonkers too check it  out if you haven't seen it i  highly recommend it adventure of  nebulandia adventure of nebulandia  is a one-piece tv special that aired  almost exactly five years ago to the day  i've praised different shows and films  for starting with a bang but  this one does that on acid the very  first scene shows the straw hats  arriving at a location before being  bombarded and attacked  showing footage of luffy dying  and when faced with this enormous army  of cgi marines how will the straw hats  contend with the three different  character models it has i'm serious  count them there's like three  jokes aside because of this opening  there's a large portion of the story  that is dedicated to a flashback which  means that to a degree there's an  element of dramatic irony  here we know where the story is going  but the characters don't and in this  case the tv special elected to swerve us  as well as the straw hats when the  climactic event actually does get  underway we think it's going one way but  it actually goes a different way unlike  the prior film which occupies a very  specific period of time during one piece  of story  this much like the rest of the films  arbitrarily and vaguely slots itself in  with  all the grace i would expect from a tv  special they're sailing past an island  and would you look who it is  it's foxy remember him  no well neither does luffy but boy is he  mad and dagnammit does he wants some  payback from that last time luffy gave  him the wham bam rubber man knockout oh  yeah  the foxy pirates in this film feels sort  of like a sideshow novelty act that  through flashbacks a few funny moments  and some clever placement of nostalgic  throwbacks  integrate themselves as the primary  antagonistic force within this narrative  early on  and they are sort of i'll explain you  see there's some difference with this  foxy crew compared to their days with  the davey back fight and that's the  addition of two new crewmates  dojaku an experienced shipwright and  komei  a former marine the plot early on  follows in the same spirit as the  original arc of long ring long land the  foxy pirates propose a rematch of their  davey back fight with the first round  being an eating competition  however thankfully the plot takes an  interesting turn away from where i  thought it was going as sanji luffy and  zoro all fall victim to the master plan  put in place by the two new recruits of  the foxy pirates double-crossing their  own crew  they now have the straw hats in place to  eat these mushrooms  that basically make them all weak and  unmotivated to be honest i thought this  was going to be a really bad special  focusing largely on the davy back fight  but it turned itself around in the first  act as it instead of being a rematch  with foxy  becomes an unexpected rescue mission  where the typically less combative  characters aside from frankie and robin  i guess have to now  save the rest of their crew it turns out  the two new recruits on the foxy pirates  are both marines supported by the world  government  specifically komei who he himself is a  vice admiral of the navy  essentially their plan is remove the  straw hats from the picture once and for  all there's some more surprises in this  special but i'll leave it there  in case anyone wants to check it out  because it's kind of good for those of  you that enjoy the world building there  are some cool elements here and there to  this one too  now with luffy sanji and zorro in tow  the vice admiral and his subordinates  take this cargo of prisoners to an  island called  nebulandia oh my god he said it he said  it  this island incorporates some really  cool stuff like a giant plant that eats  devil fruit folk but what i liked most  about this was the sea water mist that  envelops the area zapping everyone who  has devil fruit abilities of their  powers which i honestly really enjoyed  as it meant that luffy had to rely on  his friends more than he might  normally it's a rather simple mechanic  but it made an interesting difference to  the story seeing as it usually is  all the powers are none of the powers  along with no energy because the sea  water usually kills them or tries to  kill them at least  from an animation front i thought it  looked fairly standard with moments that  looked rather great and something i  thought was nice was how the film covers  many different color palettes  adding variety to the film though i'm  not personally a fan of the over  saturated color palettes that this film  bathes in additionally while i was happy  that the foxy pirates facilitated an  early swerve in the film  i wasn't a massive fan of their  participation throughout and to be  honest  thought someone else could have been  more fun to tag along with the straw hat  crew  overall i thought the film itself was  interesting in parts with some moments  that operated around some really cool  ideas and surprises the special itself  isn't amazing but it has more hits than  misses for me worth checking out if  you're a fan of the series  adventure in the ocean's navel while  sounding like an average nautical themed  adult fetish film  adventures in the ocean's navel is also  the title of the very first one-piece tv  special released in the year 2000 this  is thankfully only 49 minutes in length  and operates with a really simple story  there's a big hole in the ocean luffy  and co want to get the treasure inside  and that's pretty much the gist of this  one but  naturally things are a little more  complicated than they previously thought  once they get into this big ol hole this  entire special has a really nostalgic  vibe to it and one that i really really  liked  similar to the very first movie of the  one piece series i covered  it only has the east blue crew prior to  ever recruiting chopper or anyone else  thereafter  its animation is nostalgic humble and  quaint and it starts with luffy peeing  off the side of the ship  funny imagery aside however this begins  with a really characterful and funny set  of scenes  luffy is fishing for something big off  the side nami asks what he's doing and  then something big begins pulling the  ship  luffy thinking it's a massive catch  starts pulling everything and the ship  along to that destination while nami and  everyone wonder what the heck is going  on they finally reached the end and it's  a treasure chest luffy  immeasurably disappointed throws the  chest away citing that it tasted  awful prompting nami to then jump into  the water after it getting angry at  luffy for throwing it away  i loved this interaction because it only  works if you understand these characters  it's simple but it's entertaining  character writing it's compelling the  chest itself leads nami usa and sanji  down into the depths below in search of  a treasure  and just when i thought we were getting  away from this discovering a new island  trope in  every one of these standalone films what  do we get  in the depths of the ocean another  island  i actually really like the vibe in this  story it's easily the funniest out of  all of them that i've seen so far  has a great sense of exploration and  discovery while having a strong focus on  character  everything that happens in it happens as  a result of who these characters are how  do they find this location  luffy and nami being luffy and nami how  do they get down there luffy and usop  how do they survive and get discovered  sanji usaf and zaro all contributing in  their own unique way  that only makes sense if they do it this  is compelling writing 101 and while the  plot is simple  it very much is a story i can get behind  and enjoy  specifically because i'm a fan of the  series the second half of the story  however is a slightly more traditional  one-piece special in that they identify  a big bad and get to know the local  inhabitants and their struggles before  helping them thereafter they're scary  spooky skeleton zombie men pirates  talking bats and a plot involving this  young boy that's quite emotionally rich  too considering the time spent on the  actual topic  by the time i reached the end this felt  like all the best parts of thriller bark  meets the intro sequence of fishman  island crossed with indiana jones  it's fun the action isn't as strong as  the new more flashy films or specials  but the passion put into the writing for  this one is clear  it's a wonderful fun romp that isn't too  long and offers more bang for his buck  than most of these specials could even  hope to incorporate  i'm a huge fan of this one check it out  episode of sabo  if any of you out there someday decide  to tackle all of the one piece tv  specials like i did  you will no doubt come into contact  quite quickly with the likes of what i  call  the episode of series there are six one  piece tv specials affectionately titled  the episode of nami luffy the east blue  the mary's  skypia and in this case sabo every one  of these details previously told stories  in miniature  sort of like a speedrun of a particular  arc episode of nami details the ireland  park arc episode of skype and east blue  obviously cover their respective arcs  and episode of mary covers the water  seven  in these lobby arcs however episode of  luffy and episode of sabo while covering  previously released storylines like  luffy's origin and sabo's origin  and sabo's overall participation in the  dressrosa arc they also do so in tandem  with new material which is what i will  be doing today with episode of sabo i  won't be covering episode of luffy  simply because i'm exhausted and that  special was aggressively mediocre  however episode of sabo is at the very  least something i found enjoyable while  also shedding light once again on  pockets of time we weren't present for  with sabo  as he works his way up to revealing  himself to luffy at the coliseum in  dressrosa once again this is exactly  what i am looking for in a tv special  well sort of while 3d 2y was fantastic  in that it was almost entirely new  material  this does have a considerable chunk of  time dedicated to showing material we've  already seen  and so from the get-go i should say i  won't be covering the stuff that we've  seen already in the dressrosa video i  did for the manga  if you want to see those takes go check  out those videos myself my editor worked  very hard on them  but back to this special right out the  gate in the very first scene it  demonstrates exactly why i like tv  specials as a concept  they offer a long-running series the  possibility of expanding upon different  scenes while also demonstrating  different points of view i drew  attention to a particular cover chapter  that oda drew depicting ace and  whitebeard's graves ages ago  however what i didn't notice upon first  glancing it was that there were three  sake cups set alongside ace's grave a  clear indication that saba was  in fact alive before he was ever  revealed to be so in this special we  finally get to see him place those sake  cups  something we didn't get to actually see  in the original anime or  manga and while this special does at  large deal around the same story as  dressrosa  it more importantly predominantly shows  it from a new perspective  in sabo in the original material we  first came into contact with sabo proper  when he revealed himself to luffy during  the tournament  however in this we see not only sabo but  also koala  as they exchange information covertly at  a cafe in the city this acts as a useful  vehicle to remind viewers of the current  stakes and context going on in the  special in a natural way  illuminating to us also the very instant  sabo learns about the first place prize  of the tournament  aces flame flame fruit and throughout  this while luffy and the rest of the  pieces get put into place  it's interesting getting to witness  savo's takes on each of the goings-on  leading up to his grand reveal  short as they may be as a fan of the  series i quite enjoyed seeing them  as far as animation goes i found this  film to be surprisingly weak  while the artwork was perfectly fine and  good the animation felt very limited and  clunky  with the vast majority of it barely  moving at all relying heavily on stills  and limited animation i don't think this  would bother many heck i don't think the  vast majority of people out there would  even notice without someone pointing it  out to them if they weren't into  animation in the first place but if you  are someone who enjoys great animation  as a cornerstone to your animated media  maybe sit this one out to cap off this  special there's the moment sabo acquires  the flame flame fruit with additional  dialogue and reflection on what this  means to him  before blasting the floor saving rebecca  and winning the tournament ending  the special overall while i really  didn't love this special by any means i  can at least appreciate the angle with  which it chose to deliver its story  there wasn't a ton of extra stuff added  but what little there was i did enjoy  i don't think i'd recommend this one  over the original series or even as  supplementary material to most  it's really not necessary however if you  love one piece and are looking for  something to tie you over until the next  chapter releases  maybe check it out you could enjoy it  one piece heart of gold  this film starts out pretty much by the  books and the plot itself is really  simple overall there's a little girl of  value on the marine ship  and these pirates want her the pirates  themselves that want this child make up  the villains of the story and while  trying to capture the girls she uses the  chaos that ensues around her capture as  an opportunity to escape  and by pure chance in the vast expanse  of the ocean  who does she run into but the straw hat  crew  that convenient trope aside i do  actually really enjoy this little girl  character she's a little abrasive but  she's tough and that's something a  little different than what i usually see  in these films usually it's nami or  something that needs saving the villain  in this instance is probably the least  interesting villain i've come across in  any of these specials his name is  literally and i quote  mad treasure and he is a treasure hunter  specifically trying to get a hold of  this little girl because she knows where  the pure gold substance is  in this story pure gold is a substance  that can apparently by the entire world  [Laughter]  one major difference i noticed between  these specials is the quality of  animation  implemented and the stories they tell  apart from 3d 2y which was very good and  interesting all of the other specials  are animated in a much more limited  fashion than pretty much any of the  films available for viewing once again  the artwork and character likeness is  perfectly accurate but the movement is  very limited and very little character  acting you don't even need to compare  this to stellar animated features like  movie 6  or any of the modern ones either for  this to be obvious it's clear that the  time dedicated to these wasn't on par  with the films which i suppose is  perfectly fine i guess  films are supposed to be on a larger  scale anyway one thing i will say  regarding the film's approach in terms  of animation is that at the very end of  the film the quality does pick up quite  a bit  thankfully in addition to that the  selection of scenes and settings this  special boasts are interesting too  with a large chunk of this special  taking place in the belly of a giant  anglerfish and in saying that there's  some nice imagery with the giant angler  fish at the end  as well as some nice backgrounds across  the special overall from a story  standpoint  it might be because i've now sat through  so so many  tv specials and films that i've become  jaded of all of these recycled plot  lines  but the story didn't exactly set my  world on fire it has a lot of the same  bells and whistles used in almost any of  the other one-piece films or specials  contrived beginning that hooks the crew  with the main character or villain of  interest  they look for treasure get kidnapped  fight the bad guy at the end and repeat  and while that's a very basic outline a  lot of these specials didn't offer much  in the way of anything significant  branching out from this either  and specifically when it came to these  specials when i don't have really nice  animation to carry me through the film's  unimaginative story  i tend to look more harshly upon it  though i suppose that's my own failing  with that said i genuinely thought that  there were plenty of things  to enjoy about this one and it had at  the very least some interesting  backgrounds and a scrappy kid character  that turned out to be 200 years old  however  having gone through the last month  watching the films and now these tv  specials  i can safely say that they were  significantly better than i anticipated  them to be  now that's not to say that i thought  they were oscar-worthy though i really  did like movie six  i am very impressed nevertheless with  the level of consistency one piece's  back catalog of non-canon material  really has to offer  i was fully prepared to be much more  combative and harsh with my essays but  really none of the films were so bad to  warrant such a criticism and the vast  majority i even enjoyed to a great  extent  the last time i went through a back  catalogue of a particular anime's films  and specials  was dragon ball and i had significantly  more criticisms than i had for these  and speaking of dragon ball i will be  taking a break next thursday for the  christmas or  holiday break and my next video will be  out on december 31st  and on that day i will be dropping my  long-awaited review of the morrow  arc of dragon ball super i've been  waiting to talk about this and many of  you have been waiting some time for it  but until then that'll do it for this  week's video  i've been toying that mark i'll see you  all december 31st  and thank you all so much for watching  merry christmas  happy hanukkah happy kwanzaa or whatever  you choose to celebrate  i'll see you all right before the end of  the year
 

